('discovery','code','clamp','Write a simple Python function clamp that bounds a number and explain its branches.',"def clamp(value, lower, upper):\n    \"\"\"Return value constrained to the closed interval.\n\n    The caller supplies lower and upper in increasing order.\n    Values already inside the interval are returned unchanged.\n    This function does not modify any external state.\n    \"\"\"\n    if value < lower:\n        return lower\n    if value > upper:\n        return upper\n    return value\n\n\ndef describe_example():\n    examples = [(2, 0, 5), (-3, 0, 5), (9, 0, 5)]\n    results = []\n    for value, lower, upper in examples:\n        bounded = clamp(value, lower, upper)\n        results.append((value, bounded))\n    return results\n\nThe first branch handles values below the interval, while the second handles values above it. The last return preserves an interior value."),
('discovery','code','pair_sum','Write a Python function returning adjacent pair sums from a list.',"def adjacent_sums(values):\n    \"\"\"Return a new list containing sums of neighboring values.\n\n    The input list is left unchanged. An empty list or a list\n    with only one element produces an empty result.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for index in range(len(values) - 1):\n        current = values[index]\n        following = values[index + 1]\n        result.append(current + following)\n    return result\n\n\ndef examples():\n    first = adjacent_sums([1, 2, 3, 4])\n    second = adjacent_sums([5])\n    third = adjacent_sums([])\n    return first, second, third\n\nThe loop stops before the last element so the next index remains valid. Each addition uses the original input values rather than the accumulated result."),
