('confirmation','prose','pottery','Explain how a clay bowl dries before firing.',"A newly shaped clay bowl contains water throughout its walls. If it dries too quickly, the outer surface may shrink before the inside does. This uneven movement can create cracks. The potter therefore places the bowl where air moves gently around it and protects thin edges from sudden drying. As water leaves, the clay becomes firmer and its color changes. At a partly dry stage the surface can still be trimmed carefully. Later the bowl becomes too fragile for that kind of work. The potter waits until the remaining moisture has escaped before putting it into the kiln. Dryness alone does not make it strong, because the clay has not yet been transformed by heat. The first firing changes its structure and prepares it for later glazing or use. Patience during drying helps preserve the shape that was made at the wheel."),
('confirmation','numbers','boxes','Explain the total when8 boxes hold24 pencils each and35 pencils are handed out.',"Each of the 8 boxes contains 24 pencils. Multiplying 8 by 24 gives 192 pencils. The teacher hands out 35 of them, leaving 157. One way to check the subtraction is to remove 30 first, reducing 192 to 162, and then remove the remaining 5 to obtain 157. The product can also be checked by splitting 24 into 20 and 4. Eight times 20 is 160 and eight times 4 is 32. Their sum is 192. The number handed out is smaller than the starting total, so the remaining count is positive. Empty spaces inside the boxes do not change the arithmetic. Adding the 35 distributed pencils to the 157 remaining pencils gives 192 again. The final available quantity is therefore 157 pencils."),
