('confirmation','code','running_total','Write a Python function returning running totals from an input list.',"def running_totals(values):\n    \"\"\"Return the total after each input value is added.\n\n    A separate list is created and the input remains unchanged.\n    The running sum begins at zero before the loop.\n    \"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    result = []\n    for value in values:\n        total += value\n        result.append(total)\n    return result\n\n\ndef example_results():\n    positive = running_totals([2, 3, 4])\n    mixed = running_totals([5, -2, 1])\n    empty = running_totals([])\n    return positive, mixed, empty\n\nEvery result entry records the sum at that point in the loop. Returning a fresh list avoids changing the caller's sequence, and an empty input naturally produces an empty result."),
('confirmation','code','even_items','Write a Python function collecting even integers without changing the input.',"def even_items(values):\n    \"\"\"Collect the even integers in their original order.\n\n    The input sequence is read once and is not changed.\n    Zero and negative even integers are included.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for value in values:\n        if value % 2 == 0:\n            result.append(value)\n    return result\n\n\ndef show_examples():\n    first = even_items([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\n    second = even_items([0, -2, -3])\n    third = even_items([])\n    return first, second, third\n\nThe remainder test selects numbers divisible by two. Appending only selected values preserves their order. No sorting or removal takes place inside the input list, so other code can still use the original sequence."),
